-### [Volume Rendering Pipeline](1318-volume-rendering-pipeline.md)
-The Volume Rendering Pipeline is a standard implementation structure for direct volume rendering that decomposes the image formation process into logical stages: data traversal, interpolation, gradient computation, classification, shading, and compositing. It solves the problem of mapping a 3D scalar field to a 2D image by treating each sample point along a viewing ray as a semi-transparent, reflective, and light-emitting entity. Unlike methods that fit geometric primitives to the data, this pipeline projects shaded samples directly onto the picture plane, allowing independent optimization of visual appearance (shading/classification) and geometric projection (compositing). It is distinguished from [Post-classification Shading Pipeline](7545-post-classification-shading-pipeline.md) by typically performing classification on pre-classified voxel values or resampled arrays, whereas the post-classification variant classifies interpolated samples directly.
+Provides a modular interface for efficient ray marching and differentiable volumetric rendering, allowing users to plug any user-defined radiance field into a standardized acceleration pipeline. It distinguishes itself from specialized implementations like Instant-NGP by offering a Python API built on PyTorch, which facilitates easier extension to dynamic and unbounded scenes while maintaining high performance.
 
 ### [Time-Sliced Pipelined Architecture](1435-time-sliced-pipelined-architecture.md)
-The Time-Sliced Pipelined Architecture is a hardware design methodology for volume rendering systems that achieves real-time performance by time-slicing functional blocks to allow the simultaneous processing of multiple tasks. It solves the bottleneck of sequential hardware execution by overlapping pipeline stages, enabling continuous communication between hardware components and software interfaces. Unlike general-purpose DSP-based solutions that require multiple chips for similar volumes, this architecture integrates the entire rendering pipeline into a single system using reusable sub-cores, allowing for efficient layout and configuration based on specific processing requirements.
