 ## Background
-The concept of decomposing rendering into passes originates from the need to handle complex lighting and transparency effects that cannot be achieved in a single rasterization pass. Early volume rendering pipelines, such as those described in [Volume Rendering Pipeline](1318-volume-rendering-pipeline.md), established the logical separation of data traversal, classification, and compositing [S1, unofficial]. The advent of GPU programmability allowed for the implementation of multi-pass architectures like [Custom Pass Rendering](3629-custom-pass-rendering.md) and [Screen Quad Rendering](2435-screen-quad-rendering.md), which leverage Frame Buffer Objects (FBOs) to store intermediate states [S1, S2, unofficial]. The [DirectXSplat Renderer API](8504-directxsplat-renderer-api.md) represents a modern application of these principles in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting, optimizing resource management for real-time performance [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of decomposing rendering into passes is rooted in the evolution of hardware-accelerated graphics. Early splatting methods relied on list-based accumulation, which suffered from sorting overheads and visibility artifacts [S2, unofficial]. The introduction of sheet-buffer and slice-by-slice techniques addressed these by organizing splats into cache-aligned structures [S1, S3]. Modern implementations, such as [DirectXSplat](8504-directxsplat-renderer-api.md) and [Custom Pass Rendering](3629-custom-pass-rendering.md), leverage modern GPU APIs (Direct3D 12, Vulkan/WebGPU) to manage complex multi-pass workflows efficiently [S1, S2, unofficial]. The [Time-Sliced Pipelined Architecture](1435-time-sliced-pipelined-architecture.md) represents an earlier hardware-centric approach to parallelizing these stages [S1, unofficial].
 
